<!-- Website-only teaching for android/performance/jank.md; original chapter remains read-only. Sources checked 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->

<p class="story-lead">A feed scroll catches even though no network request is running. The missing evidence is one frame: did app code run too long, wait for another thread, or finish while rendering downstream fell behind?</p>

Start from the delayed frame, follow its dependencies, then choose the smallest change that the trace supports.

<!-- @after First Principles :: visual -->

### A slow frame has several possible owners

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:3"><div class="node"><strong>Running</strong><small>App CPU work occupies the thread: inspect the expensive slice.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Waiting</strong><small>A lock, Binder call or scheduling delay prevents progress: follow the dependency.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Rendering or presentation</strong><small>Inspect RenderThread, GPU and system tracks before blaming a composable.</small></div></div><figcaption>This diagnostic board is not a measured timeline. FrameTimeline supplies expected and actual intervals on supported Android versions; a long slice alone is not a complete root cause.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Staff Engineer Thinking :: animation -->

### Investigate one late frame

<figure class="stepper"><span class="stepper-title">Investigate one late frame</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="frame"><strong>Frame</strong><small>The observed stutter</small></div><div class="node" data-el="wait"><strong>Waiter</strong><small>Main thread</small></div><div class="node" data-el="owner"><strong>Dependency</strong><small>Thread or subsystem</small></div><div class="node" data-el="repeat"><strong>Verification</strong><small>Same interaction</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Select the frame", "caption": "Find the frame associated with the stutter and align its expected and actual work. Keep the interaction and device conditions recorded.", "state": {"frame": "error"}, "add": ["frame", "wait", "owner", "repeat"]}, {"label": "Classify the time", "caption": "Check whether Main is executing app work, runnable but not scheduled, or blocked. These need different repairs.", "state": {"wait": "active"}}, {"label": "Follow the dependency", "caption": "In this illustrative case Main waits on a lock. Inspect its owner rather than shortening unrelated drawing code.", "state": {"wait": "warn", "owner": "active"}}, {"label": "Retest the interaction", "caption": "Reduce the evidenced blocking boundary, then rerun the same scroll. A lower recomposition count alone does not prove smoother presentation.", "state": {"owner": "done", "repeat": "active"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence. Use Previous, Next or Play; every step is also available as text.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Staff Engineer Thinking :: staff -->

Pair a reproducible journey with field cohorts. A benchmark can catch a repeatable regression; production data reveals which device and refresh-rate populations experience it.

<!-- @before Further Reading :: check -->

### Check your diagnosis

<details><summary>Main is runnable but rarely scheduled. Is a faster layout the established fix?</summary>

No. Investigate CPU contention and scheduling in the same interval before attributing the delay to layout.

</details>

<details><summary>How do lab and field measurements complement one another?</summary>

A repeatable lab journey helps test causes; field cohorts show which users and devices experience the issue.

</details>

<details><summary>Why keep a low-tier device in the release gate?</summary>

Its headroom and scheduling conditions can expose regressions hidden by a fast developer device.

</details>

<!-- @before Further Reading :: deeper -->

Primary evidence for the added layer: [FrameTimeline](https://perfetto.dev/docs/data-sources/frametimeline), [Frame metrics](https://developer.android.com/topic/performance/benchmarking/macrobenchmark-metrics).

